-- A SECOND, differently-shaped driving example for DESIGN.md's "Tier 3,
-- not v0" -- unlike `circular_buffer_disjoint.tr`'s RELATIONAL,
-- stateful invariant (disjointness depends on the joint history of
-- FOUR registers' writes), this one is a purely ALGEBRAIC fact about
-- two INDEX EXPRESSIONS, no state history involved at all: `write`
-- accesses `m[i]`, `read` accesses `m[7 - i]` -- for ANY integer `i` in
-- `[0, 8)`, `i` and `7 - i` can NEVER be equal, since `i = 7 - i` would
-- require `2*i = 7`, impossible for an integer (7 is odd). This holds
-- for every possible value of `i` simultaneously -- not just the ones
-- `i` happens to take on at runtime -- so it's a strictly STRONGER,
-- simpler claim than the FIFO's, provable (in principle) from `i`'s
-- declared type alone, with no need to track how `i` got there.
--
-- Confirmed empirically, not assumed: `--explain-schedule` reports
-- `rule write conflicts with rule read: write meets read on {m}`, a
-- derived stall, exactly as conservative as if `i`/`7 - i` shared no
-- relationship at all. `advance` (which both `write` and `read` also
-- depend on, for `i` itself) is deliberately isolated via two
-- `conflict_free` directives, the same pattern `mem_disjoint_ranges.tr`
-- uses for its own `bump_i`/`bump_j` -- leaving `write`/`read`'s
-- conflict on `m` as the ONE thing left unresolved, cleanly isolated
-- from any register-sharing noise.
--
-- Why today's checker can't close this: `schedule.rs`'s `IndexForm`
-- recognizes a base plus a CONSTANT multiplier and offset (`k*base +
-- c`), composed through `+`/`-`/`*` -- but `7 - i` isn't `i` scaled by
-- a constant, it's a constant MINUS `i` (`i` negated, then offset) --
-- a shape `index_form`'s own recursion doesn't handle today (confirmed:
-- this file's `write`/`read` are NOT reported disjoint, unlike
-- `mem_disjoint_rw.tr`'s literal addresses or `mem_disjoint_affine.tr`'s
-- `base + k` shape). Unlike `circular_buffer_disjoint.tr`, this gap
-- does NOT need real dependent/refinement types or relational
-- reasoning across multiple registers -- it needs `IndexForm` extended
-- with a NEGATION case (`c - base` as `multiplier = -1, offset = c`)
-- plus a parity argument (`base` and `c - base` coincide only when `c`
-- is even, at `base = c/2`; for odd `c` -- as here, `c = 7` -- they
-- NEVER coincide, for ANY `base`). A meaningfully SMALLER, more
-- tractable slice of "tier 3" than the FIFO's own case: an `IndexForm`
-- extension, not a new proof engine.
module MirroredIndex {
    mem m : [8][8]

    reg i : [4] where i < 8 = 0

    in x : [8]
    out y : [8] = 0

    rule write {
        m[i] := x
    }

    rule read {
        y := m[7 - i]
    }

    rule advance {
        if i < 7 {
            i := (i + 1).!
        } else {
            i := 0
        }
    }

    schedule {
        conflict_free { write, advance }
        conflict_free { read, advance }
    }
}
